import{_ as t,o,c as n,ag as s}from"./chunks/framework.2cFhd8Ux.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Sandboxes: Codespaces and Dev Containers","description":"","frontmatter":{"source":"setup/sandboxes.md"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"setup/sandboxes.md","filePath":"setup/sandboxes.md"}'),a={name:"setup/sandboxes.md"};function r(i,e,d,h,c,l){return o(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="sandboxes-codespaces-and-dev-containers" tabindex="-1">Sandboxes: Codespaces and Dev Containers <a class="header-anchor" href="#sandboxes-codespaces-and-dev-containers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sandboxes: Codespaces and Dev Containers&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Where to run an agent on code you didn&#39;t write, and exactly what that protects you from.</p></blockquote><p>Read this before week 2, when you first point an agent at the class codebase. It applies safety contract rules <a href="./safety-contract.html#3-untrusted-code-runs-in-a-sandbox">3</a> and <a href="./safety-contract.html#4-no-real-secrets-near-an-agent">4</a>.</p><p><strong>Where you run an agent decides the worst case.</strong> An agent reads files and runs commands with your permissions. On your laptop, the worst case is your laptop: your home folder, SSH keys, browser sessions, every other repository. In a sandbox, the worst case is the sandbox. The course treats three things as <strong>untrusted code</strong>: the class codebase and any other repository you didn&#39;t write, other teams&#39; forks (the PR round), and the <code>injection-lab</code> kit, which is hostile on purpose.</p><h2 id="the-options" tabindex="-1">The options <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-options" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The options&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Sandbox</th><th>What it is</th><th>Use it for</th><th>Cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>GitHub Codespaces</strong> (default)</td><td>A cloud VM running VS Code, created from a repository</td><td>Every lab; anything untrusted</td><td>Free monthly hours (<a href="./../TOOLS.html#free-infrastructure">Tool Handout: Free infrastructure</a>)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dev container on your laptop</strong></td><td>A Docker container described by the repository&#39;s <code>.devcontainer/devcontainer.json</code>, opened with VS Code&#39;s Dev Containers extension</td><td>When Codespaces hours run out; when you need the lab machine on the campus network</td><td>Docker Desktop is free for educational use</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Docker Sandboxes</strong> (<code>sbx</code>)</td><td>Each agent in its own microVM, on your laptop</td><td>Running a CLI agent with stronger isolation than a container</td><td>Free locally</td></tr><tr><td><strong>The agent&#39;s own sandbox mode</strong></td><td>For example Copilot&#39;s local sandboxing, switched on with <code>/sandbox enable</code></td><td>An extra layer inside one of the above, never instead of it</td><td>Free; off by default</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The same <code>.devcontainer/devcontainer.json</code> drives both Codespaces and a local dev container, so a repository that works in one works in the other.</p><h2 id="running-an-agent-on-untrusted-code-step-by-step" tabindex="-1">Running an agent on untrusted code, step by step <a class="header-anchor" href="#running-an-agent-on-untrusted-code-step-by-step" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Running an agent on untrusted code, step by step&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>Get the code from its URL</strong>, never from a folder someone sent you. A received folder (zip, shared drive, USB stick) can carry a <code>.git/config</code> that makes git run an attacker&#39;s program the first time any tool runs <code>git status</code>, before an agent&#39;s approval prompt appears (<a href="./../resources/case-studies.html#a6-received-folders-code-that-runs-before-the-prompt">case study A6</a>). If you must open a received folder, read <code>.git/config</code> and list <code>.git/hooks/</code> first, with the agent closed.</li><li><strong>Create the codespace with no secrets attached.</strong> In Settings → Codespaces, your secrets list which repositories may use them; a repository with untrusted content gets none except the agent&#39;s own free-tier model key (no payment method, so worth nothing to steal). No GitHub token with write access, no other provider&#39;s key.</li><li><strong>Read the dev container config before you build it.</strong> <code>devcontainer.json</code> can run commands (<code>postCreateCommand</code>, features, a Dockerfile) when the container is created. Someone else&#39;s config is someone else&#39;s code (<a href="./safety-contract.html#8-read-before-you-adopt">contract rule 8</a>).</li><li><strong>Read the agent instructions before the agent does.</strong> <code>AGENTS.md</code>, <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, <code>.cursor/rules</code>, <code>.github/copilot-instructions.md</code>, skills folders and MCP configs in the repository are instructions to your agent, written by a stranger. An agent can be told to start an MCP server declared in an untrusted repository&#39;s config (<a href="./../TOOLS.html#not-verified">Tool Handout: Not verified</a> lists the advisory, unverified). Read them; disable what you didn&#39;t write.</li><li><strong>Keep approvals on</strong> for every command and every write (<a href="./safety-contract.html#2-approvals-stay-on">rule 2</a>).</li><li><strong>Commit or stash before the agent starts</strong>, on a branch, so you can see and undo everything it did (<a href="./safety-contract.html#7-save-points-before-and-after">rule 7</a>).</li><li><strong>Stop the codespace when you finish</strong>, and delete it when the task is merged.</li></ol><h2 id="what-a-sandbox-does-and-doesnt-protect" tabindex="-1">What a sandbox does and doesn&#39;t protect <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-a-sandbox-does-and-doesnt-protect" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What a sandbox does and doesn&#39;t protect&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A sandbox moves the blast radius; it doesn&#39;t remove it. Anthropic&#39;s engineering team puts it this way: both <strong>filesystem isolation</strong> and <strong>network isolation</strong> are needed, because without either one a compromised agent can escape or exfiltrate (<a href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/claude-code-sandboxing" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Anthropic</a>). A Codespace gives you the first, not the second.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Asset</th><th>Protected in a Codespace or dev container?</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Your laptop&#39;s files, SSH keys, browser sessions</td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td><td>They aren&#39;t in the sandbox (unless you mount them into a dev container: don&#39;t)</td></tr><tr><td>Your other repositories&#39; files</td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td><td>Only this repository is cloned</td></tr><tr><td>Secrets attached to the sandbox (Codespaces secrets, <code>.env</code>)</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td>The agent can read them with <code>env</code> or <code>cat</code>. Attach none where untrusted content is read</td></tr><tr><td>The repository itself</td><td><strong>Partly</strong></td><td>The codespace holds a token that can push to its repository. Branch protection on <code>main</code> and your pushed history limit the damage</td></tr><tr><td>Data sent out over the network</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td>Codespaces have open outbound internet. A filter in the harness (week 4 builds one) is a speed bump; only network isolation enforced outside the agent stops exfiltration</td></tr><tr><td>Your free API allowance</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td>A runaway loop spends it from inside the sandbox. <code>mini-agent</code> has a step limit for this reason</td></tr><tr><td>Your GitHub account</td><td><strong>Partly</strong></td><td>Only as far as the tokens inside the sandbox reach. Scope them (<a href="./api-keys.html#scoped-tokens">API Keys, Tokens and Spending Caps: Scoped tokens</a>)</td></tr><tr><td>Correctness of what gets merged</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td>That&#39;s verification and review, weeks 6–9</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Network isolation, in practice.</strong> When a step needs no network (running a received repository&#39;s tests after dependencies are installed, for example), run it in a container with networking off:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">docker</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> run</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --rm</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --network</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> none</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -v</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">$PWD</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;:/work</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -w</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> /work</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> node:22</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> npm</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> test</span></span></code></pre></div><p>An agent needs the network to reach its model, so it can&#39;t simply run offline. The course&#39;s layers, from weakest to strongest: an <strong>egress filter</strong> in the harness that inspects commands (week 4; the <code>injection-lab</code> kit documents how encodings, variables and other tools get past a string filter), <strong>removing the tools</strong> that can send data out from any agent that reads untrusted text, and <strong>no secrets in reach</strong>. If there&#39;s nothing worth stealing and no tool to send it with, prompt injection has much less to work with.</p><h2 id="dev-containers-on-your-laptop" tabindex="-1">Dev containers on your laptop <a class="header-anchor" href="#dev-containers-on-your-laptop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Dev containers on your laptop&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When Codespaces hours run out, or when you need to reach the lab machine on the campus network:</p><ol><li>Install Docker Desktop (free for education) and VS Code&#39;s <strong>Dev Containers</strong> extension.</li><li>Clone the repository from its URL.</li><li>Read <code>.devcontainer/devcontainer.json</code> (step 3 above).</li><li>Command Palette → <strong>Dev Containers: Reopen in Container</strong>.</li><li>Don&#39;t add mounts of your home folder, <code>~/.ssh</code> or <code>~/.config</code> to <code>devcontainer.json</code>. If the config already has them, remove them.</li></ol><p>A container shares your laptop&#39;s kernel, so it is weaker isolation than a VM. For a hostile repository (<code>injection-lab</code>, a stranger&#39;s fork), prefer a Codespace or Docker Sandboxes.</p><h2 id="never-on-the-lab-machine" tabindex="-1">Never on the lab machine <a class="header-anchor" href="#never-on-the-lab-machine" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Never on the lab machine&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Students use the lab machine&#39;s model API, not its shell. Never run an agent, a received repository or a test suite on the lab machine itself: one compromised session there affects the whole class&#39;s queue.</p><p>Back to <a href="./">Week 0</a> · Agent safety rules: <a href="./safety-contract.html">Agent Safety Contract</a> · Problems: <a href="./../resources/troubleshooting.html#my-codespace-wont-start-or-im-out-of-hours">troubleshooting</a></p>',22)])])}const g=t(a,[["render",r]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
